    "title": "US Politics Update",
    "text": "• Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu is meeting with President Donald Trump on Tuesday.\n• The meeting is the first since the two leaders launched a joint war against Iran.\n• Netanyahu plans to smooth out strains in his relationship with Trump.\n• The two leaders will discuss the framework agreement the U.S. and Israel signed with Lebanon over the Israel-Hezbollah war.\n• They will also discuss the expansion of the Abraham Accords.\n• Trump is meeting with Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy on Tuesday.\n• The meeting is ahead of Sen. Lindsey Graham's funeral.\n• Trump is expected to deliver remarks at the funeral.\n• Senate is expected to confirm Jay Clayton as the next intelligence director on Tuesday.\n• Clayton is expected to be confirmed along party lines.\n• Security around the White House is tight due to Zelenskyy and Netanyahu visits.\n• Only foot traffic is allowed on streets surrounding the complex.\n• Vehicular traffic is blocked by city police cars and dump trucks at key intersections.\n• Netanyahu is staying at Blair House, the U.S. government's guest house across from the White House.\n• Zelenskyy has the first meeting with Trump, scheduled for 9:30 a.m., followed by Netanyahu at 11 a.m.\n• Zelenskyy will visit Trump at the White House before Sen. Graham's funeral.\n• Trump and Zelenskyy will meet Tuesday ahead of Sen. Lindsey Graham's funeral.\n• Their meeting is their second sit-down this month.\n• Zelenskyy posted on X early Tuesday that he had arrived in the U.S. and listed the goals for his visit.\n• The Ukrainian leader's number one priority is anti-ballistic defense and strategic cooperation with America.",
    "summary": "Clayton is expected to be confirmed along party lines after a contentious hearing two weeks ago in which he repeatedly refused to say that former President Joe Biden won the 2020 election. Still, Democrats have said they have more confidence in Clayton than they do in Trump’s temporary pick for the job, Bill Pulte, who has no known intelligence experience and investigated the president’s perceived enemies in his previous post as a federal housing regulator.",
